-# Intelligence Regulation
+# Intelligence Regulation: zero-knowledge proofs of LLM inference
 
-The ZKP prototype has complete small-block proofs and private zkVM receipts
-for a synthetic two-block token computation. Trained-model integration,
-larger-profile privacy and complete-model performance remain open. See
-[current project status](docs/zkmatrix-poc/MVP_STATUS.md).
+**Status: research prototype. Not audited. Not production software.**
 
-For the local Tech Week demonstration, run
-`python3 scripts/zk60_techweek_server.py` and open <http://127.0.0.1:8765>.
-It verifies real saved proofs, rejects tampered artifacts, and offers live
-synthetic-block proving. [Demo scope, prerequisites and presentation guide](docs/zkmatrix-poc/ZK60_TECHWEEK_DEMO.md).
+This repository holds a working prover and verifier that show, in zero knowledge, that
+a language model with **public weights** produced a stated result, while the **input,
+the output and every intermediate activation stay hidden**. The main prover
+(`hashline/`) is a hash-based proof system written in Rust over the Goldilocks field.
+A second, slower route re-executes small models inside the RISC Zero zkVM and wraps
+the result in a Groth16 receipt.
+
+<MANIFESTO: pending Victor>
+
+## 1. What it is
+
+- **Privacy scope "P1"** (decision D1): the proof hides the inputs, outputs and
+  activations. The weights are public. A later scope, "P3", would also hide the
+  weights; it is not built.
+- **What a proof says:** "this public model, with these pinned weights and this
+  registered policy, gave this public answer on some hidden input". For the
+  classifier, the public answer is one verdict bit plus the input's length in tokens
+  (D45).
+- **How:** every transformer block (norms, attention with an exact integer softmax,
+  RoPE, the SiLU feed-forward, residuals) is written as integer arithmetic. Lookup
+  arguments, sumchecks and a hiding polynomial commitment prove it. One proof under
+  one transcript covers the whole run. The verifier rebuilds every public table from
+  the pinned public weights.
+
+## 2. What works, with numbers and scope
+
+All numbers were measured once (n = 1) on one development laptop: a 22-thread CPU,
+15 GB of RAM and an RTX 4070 Laptop GPU with 8 GB. All proofs below ran on the CPU.
+They are research measurements, not benchmarks.
+
+| What was proved | Prove | Verify (fresh process) | Receipt | Soundness floor |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **Shieldstral-1.0-3B safety-classifier verdict** on a real 17-token post (one proof per category: sexual, violence, hate) | about 456 s per category with 8 threads | about 36 s | 848 MB | 96 bits |
+| **TinyLlama-1.1B next token**, 32-token hidden prompt, all 22 trained blocks | 85 s | 11 s | 255 MB | 97 bits |
+| **Llama-3-8B next token**, 128-token hidden prompt, all 32 blocks | about 500 s end to end | about 57 s | 928 MB | 96 bits |
+
+Llama-3-8B results: **Built with Meta Llama 3.** No Llama weights or derived files are
+distributed here (see `NOTICE`).
+
+- The reference prover is the frozen **token round 16b** tree. Its manifest has 301
+  files and list sha256 `04d94bc0…`, in `evidence/zk60_token_io_r1_20261017/`.
+  Round 16 was accepted on soundness with fixes (`evidence/…/REVIEW_R16.md`).
+  Round 16b's own independent check had not been done when this snapshot was made.
+- The privacy gate (`privacy-gate/`, frozen v23) re-derived all 442 public tables of
+  the real classifier proof and found nothing disclosed on any hidden-data
+  commitment. That is gate evidence, not a privacy acceptance.
+- Speed is far from the project's target of **at least 50 proved tokens per second for
+  a private 8B model on a high-end GPU**. Today's private 8B rate is well under 1 token
+  per second. See `docs/zkmatrix-poc/MVP_STATUS.md` and
+  `docs/zkmatrix-poc/ZK60_THROUGHPUT_TARGET.md`.
+
+## 3. What does not work yet
+
+- **Privacy acceptances are narrow.** The two accepted privacy statements (D33 and
+  D36: TinyLlama-1.1B at 32 tokens) cover named binaries and trees only, recorded up
+  to round frozen-r11. The **classifier privacy statement is a draft**
+  (`docs/zkmatrix-poc/ZK60_PRIVACY_STATEMENT_CLASSIFIER_SHIELDSTRAL_T128_DRAFT_v3.md`)
+  and has not been accepted.
+- **The zkVM route rests on recorded deviations:** about 97 bits of conjectured
+  soundness (D3) and a Groth16 trusted setup (D4). Its receipts cover synthetic tiny
+  models only.
+- **No external audit** of any part.
+- **Checking a published classifier receipt is not yet self-service.** The verifier
+  rebuilds its tables from the pinned Shieldstral weights. The tied-table step reads the
+  checkpoint from a fixed development-box path unless the export names one (derive.rs
+  `DEFAULT_CHECKPOINT`). That file is pinned by the privacy gate, so it was not edited
+  for this release. An override is planned for a reviewed round.
+- **Rebuilding the model exports from this tree is not self-contained yet.** The
+  export and calibration tools (`tools/model-export/`) need a few upstream files that
+  are not in this snapshot. They are listed in `tools/model-export/README.md`. Each
+  tool also records its own digest in the export, so a rebuilt export differs in its
+  provenance fields from the published ones.
+- The GPU path (`--features gpu-cuda`) exists but has no accepted private-proof result.
+- The app (`apps/proven-media/`) is a testnet demo. It refuses to run outside its
+  reviewed configuration.
+
+## 4. Quick start (CPU, no model needed)
+
+Requirements: Linux, Rust 1.97 or newer (stable), Python 3.10 or newer with `numpy`,
+and about 12 GB of free RAM for the full native test suite.
+
+```sh
+# build the prover (add --offline if your cargo registry cache is already populated)
+cargo build --release --locked --manifest-path hashline/Cargo.toml
+
+# the native test suite: about 13 minutes, peak about 10 GiB of RAM.
+# The tests need the shipped synthetic loader fixtures, and a scratch directory
+# that is OUTSIDE any git work tree.
+mkdir -p ~/zk60-scratch
+cd hashline
+TMPDIR=~/zk60-scratch HL_TIO_FIXTURE_DIR=~/zk60-scratch \
+HL_R13_LOADER_FIXTURES=$PWD/../results/zk60_1b_native_20261017/r13/loader-fixtures \
+  cargo test --release --locked
+cd ..
 
-## Description: The world is racing to build Intelligence as quickly as it can. But this intelligence isn't being built to serve people, its being built because if we don't build it they will and they will win. This is a recipe for dissaster. Very quickly tragedy will strike and the world will cry out for AI to pause and only then will it become clear that short of destroying all fabs worldwide, we have no pause button for AI. Some might think its ok, we just need to build it out to be beneficial to all, but unfortunately even if AI never misbehaves, companies and governments, if left to allow runnaway competition to continue will have to make the choice between serving their people and falling behind in the AI race or neglecting the people. Eventually, AI developement must be monitored on a global scale by a single governing organization. This is the only way to remove the competition pressures that are driving humanity towards catastrophe. The only question we have left to answer is how can we establish a worldwide AI and intelligence governance system that is equitable for all countries.
